---
/*
  THE CART (2026-08-16, section 129) — the OWNER's call, and it replaces the
  order page section 126 built.

  WHAT HE ASKED FOR, in his words: choose a size on a Creature's page and add it
  to a cart; a cart icon in the header, always visible, with a count; and from
  there a normal checkout. He has seen the page that listed every piece at once
  and does not want it. That page is deleted rather than left standing.

  CARD ONLY, SINCE 2026-10-10. The cart used to end in a form that emailed the
  order to him and settled the money in the reply; it now ends in one button
  that goes to Stripe, which asks for the name, the email, the phone and the
  address there. That e-mail path, the single-piece order form and the endpoint
  behind both are gone, on the owner's decision.

  WHAT A CART COSTS ON THIS SITE, stated plainly because it is the whole reason
  this page is shaped the way it is. A cart is per-visitor mutable state that
  has to survive navigation. This site is static HTML on a CDN: there is no
  session, no per-request render and no database. That leaves three ways to hold
  such state, and two are not available here:

  - LOCALSTORAGE. Needs JavaScript. Every page stays static.
  - A COOKIE READ BY THE SERVER. Needs every cart-bearing page rendered per
    request, which means routing the whole site through a Function and giving up
    the static CDN model, the deploy hook and the URL. That is an infrastructure
    decision, not a shopping-basket one.
  - THE URL. Would work with no JavaScript, but only if every "add" link on
    every page already carried the current basket — and a static build cannot
    know a visitor's basket when it writes those links.

  So: A CART REQUIRES JAVASCRIPT, and so does a count that follows a reader from
  page to page. There is no version of either without it, short of changing how
  the site is served. The owner was told this before it was built and accepted
  it.

  WITHOUT A SCRIPT NOTHING CAN BE BOUGHT, and the page says so rather than
  pretending. Until the e-mail path went, a reader with scripts off could still
  order from a piece's own plain HTML form; that form went with it, so buying
  now needs JavaScript on this page and on the piece's. Everything else on the
  site still renders and navigates without one. The cart control in the chrome
  and the add control on a piece are HIDDEN rather than dead in that case,
  because a control that cannot do its job is worse than no control.

  THE PRICES ARE THE BUILD'S, NEVER THE FORM'S. Every figure on this page comes
  from the catalogue below, written by the build from the same query the shop
  renders. The total the reader sees is a convenience computed from those
  figures; the AUTHORITATIVE total is computed by the endpoint from
  /order-catalogue.json, from the same function, because a total computed from
  what arrives in a POST is a total the buyer wrote.
*/
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TextSurface from "../../components/TextSurface.astro";
import {getGarments, getSiteSettings} from "../../lib/content";
import {isOrderable, toOrderItem} from "../../lib/order";
import {DESTINATIONS, SHIPPING} from "../../lib/shipping";
import {imageUrl} from "../../lib/image";
import {LOCALES, localePath, pick, toLocale} from "../../lib/locales";
import {t} from "../../i18n/ui";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return LOCALES.map((lang) => ({params: {lang}}));
}

const locale = toLocale(Astro.params.lang);
const garments = await getGarments();
const settings = await getSiteSettings();

/*
  THE TERMS, BEFORE THE FORM RATHER THAN AFTER IT (2026-08-16, section 130).

  The single-piece order page has shown these two lines above its fields since
  section 104, and the cart shipped without them — so the checkout that takes
  the LARGER order told a buyer less about shipping and returns than the one
  that takes a single piece. Found by reading the two pages side by side as a
  buyer rather than by testing either.

  All three are HIS facts: ships worldwide, free over 500 euro, a return is
  arranged by writing. Nothing here is invented.
*/
const shippingLines = [
  pick(settings.shippingFree, locale),
  pick(settings.shippingReturns, locale),
].filter((line): line is string => Boolean(line));

/*
  THE WHOLE ORDERABLE CATALOGUE, EMBEDDED. The cart holds slugs; this is what
  turns a slug into a name, a price, the sizes he cuts and how many exist. It is
  the same list `/order-catalogue.json` serves, from the same function, plus the
  two things a page needs and a till roll does not: where the piece lives and
  what it looks like.

  A PIECE THAT LEAVES THE SHOP LEAVES THE CART. If he marks something sold or
  takes its price off, it is absent here, the line silently disappears from a
  reader's cart, and the endpoint would have refused to price it anyway. That is
  the correct direction to fail in: the alternative is a cart that keeps
  offering something the brand cannot sell.
*/
const items = garments.filter(isOrderable).map((g) => {
  const [frame] = g.media ?? [];
  return {
    ...toOrderItem(g),
    href: localePath(locale, `creature/${g.slug}`),
    /* small: a cart line is a check that you chose the right object, not a view */
    image: frame ? imageUrl(frame.poster, 240, 62) : null,
  };
});

const catalogueJson = JSON.stringify(items).replace(/</g, "\\u003c");

/*
  THE COUNTRIES, NAMED BY THE PLATFORM RATHER THAN BY US.

  `Intl.DisplayNames` turns an ISO code into "Giappone" or "Japan" from data the
  runtime already ships, so there is no table of sixty-odd country names to
  write twice, keep in step and get subtly wrong in one language. Built here, at
  build time, because the list cannot change between one visitor and the next.

  Sorted by the NAME as that language renders it, not by the code: a reader
  scanning for Germany looks under G in English and under D in Italian.
*/
const countryName = new Intl.DisplayNames([locale === "it" ? "it-IT" : "en-GB"], {type: "region"});
const destinations = DESTINATIONS.map((code) => ({code, name: countryName.of(code) ?? code})).sort(
  (a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name, locale === "it" ? "it-IT" : "en-GB"),
);

/*
  ONE PLACE THE CURRENCY IS DECIDED, and it follows the pieces rather than the
  page: every Creature carries its own, they are all EUR today, and hard-coding
  the symbol would be a lie the first time one is not.
*/
const currency = items[0]?.currency ?? "EUR";
---

<Base locale={locale} title={t(locale, "cart")} noindex>
  <TextSurface class="flex flex-col gap-u5 pt-u13 pb-u8">
    <h1 class="type-display text-display">{t(locale, "cart")}</h1>
  </TextSurface>

  <TextSurface class="pb-u21">
    {/*
      WITH NO SCRIPT THIS IS THE WHOLE PAGE. It is not a fallback bolted on
      afterwards: it is what the document says before anything runs, and the
      script hides it by putting `js` on <html> in the head. So a reader with
      scripts off never sees a control appear and then fail — they see a
      sentence and a way on.
    */}
    <div class="cart-noscript">
      <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "cartNeedsScript")}</p>
    </div>

    {/* Written by the script, from localStorage, against the catalogue above. */}
    <div class="cart-live flex flex-col gap-u8" hidden>
      {/*
        BACK FROM STRIPE, PAID. The card path leaves this site and returns to
        this same page with ?paid=1, which is the only moment the basket is
        known to have been bought — so it is the only place it can be emptied,
        because the cart lives in the visitor's localStorage and no server can
        reach it.
        Without this a buyer's basket still holds everything they just paid
        for, the count in the chrome still says three, and the obvious next act
        is to buy it again.
      */}
      <div data-cart-paid class="flex flex-col gap-u5" hidden>
        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "cartPaid")}</p>
        <a
          href={localePath(locale, "creature")}
          class="type-label text-body hairline-under self-start"
        >
          {t(locale, "allCreatures")}
        </a>
      </div>

      <div data-cart-empty class="flex flex-col gap-u5" hidden>
        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "cartEmpty")}</p>
        <a
          href={localePath(locale, "creature")}
          class="type-label text-body hairline-under self-start"
        >
          {t(locale, "allCreatures")}
        </a>
      </div>

      <form
        method="POST"
        action="/api/checkout"
        class="flex flex-col gap-u8"
        data-cart-form
        hidden
      >
        {/*
          THE CARD PATH DID NOT START. Shown on the way back from a refused
          /api/checkout, and it says the one thing a buyer needs first: nothing
          was charged. The basket is untouched, so trying again is one press.
        */}
        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose" data-cart-problem hidden>
          {t(locale, "cartPayProblem")}
        </p>

        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "cartIntro")}</p>

        <h2 class="type-label text-label">{t(locale, "cartPieces")}</h2>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-u5" data-cart-list></ul>

        {/*
          THE TOTAL IS A CONVENIENCE, NOT A PRICE. It adds up the figures printed
          beside it, all of which came out of the build. The authoritative figure
          is computed by the endpoint from /order-catalogue.json, which a buyer
          cannot edit. `aria-live` so a screen reader hears it change rather than
          having to go and look.
        */}
        <p class="type-mono text-mono flex gap-u3">
          <span>{t(locale, "cartTotal")}</span>
          <output data-cart-total aria-live="polite"></output>
        </p>

        <div class="type-body text-body flex flex-col gap-u1">
          {shippingLines.map((line) => <p class="max-w-prose">{line}</p>)}
          {/* Il testo intero, per chi lo vuole prima di comprare. Sezione 137. */}
          <a href={localePath(locale, "terms")} class="type-label text-label self-start hairline-under">
            {t(locale, "returnsLink")}
          </a>
        </div>
        {/*
          WHERE IT GOES, ASKED HERE AND NOT ON STRIPE'S PAGE.

          Stripe's hosted checkout collects the address AFTER the session
          exists and its own documentation says flatly that it cannot reprice
          shipping from it. So the destination is asked for before leaving, the
          rate is computed from it, and `allowed_countries` is locked to this
          answer so the address they type has to be the address they were
          priced for.

          It is worth more than the workaround it is: a buyer in Japan sees
          their real shipping figure on this page, before committing to
          anything, which no version that asked later could do.

          REQUIRED, now that paying is the only way to order: there is no path
          left that does not need to know where the parcel goes.
        */}
        <div class="field" data-destination-field hidden>
          <label class="type-label text-label" for="destination">{t(locale, "cartDestination")}</label>
          <select id="destination" name="destination" data-destination required>
            <option value="">{t(locale, "cartDestinationChoose")}</option>
            {destinations.map((d) => <option value={d.code}>{d.name}</option>)}
          </select>
        </div>

        {/*
          The figure, before they leave. Blank until a country is chosen, because
          there is no honest number to print before that.
        */}
        <p class="type-mono text-mono flex gap-u3" data-shipping-line hidden>
          <span>{t(locale, "cartShipping")}</span>
          <output data-shipping-out aria-live="polite"></output>
        </p>
        <p class="type-mono text-mono flex gap-u3" data-topay-line hidden>
          <span>{t(locale, "cartToPay")}</span>
          <output data-topay-out aria-live="polite"></output>
        </p>

        {/*
          LA RIGA SUL TRATTAMENTO DEI DATI (2026-08-17, sezione 137). Sta qui,
          sopra il pulsante e sotto i campi, perché è il punto in cui il lettore
          ha appena scritto il proprio nome e la propria email e sta per
          mandarli. Nostra, non dell'avvocato, e il link porta al suo testo.

          NON È UNA CASELLA DA SPUNTARE, ed è una decisione. Il consenso non è
          la base giuridica di questo trattamento — l'informativa dice misure
          precontrattuali e contratto — quindi chiedere una spunta di consenso
          sarebbe sbagliato nel merito oltre che un ostacolo in più fra una
          persona e il capo che vuole. Quello che serve è l'informazione, prima
          dell'invio, dove non si può non vederla.
        */}
        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">
          {t(locale, "consentLine")}{" "}
          <a href={localePath(locale, "privacy")} class="hairline-under">{t(locale, "consentLink")}</a>
        </p>

        <input type="hidden" name="lang" value={locale} />
        {/* This browser's id for the 1 of 1 lock: written by the script, see _holds.ts. */}
        <input type="hidden" name="holder" value="" data-holder />

        {/*
          THE ONLY BUTTON. It goes to Stripe, which charges nothing until the
          buyer confirms on Stripe's own page. Enter in a field never presses it
          (see the script): starting a payment is always a deliberate press.
        */}
        <button
          type="submit"
          class="type-label text-body hairline-under self-start"
          data-pay-button
          data-sending={t(locale, "sending")}
          hidden
        >
          {t(locale, "cartPay")}
        </button>
      </form>
    </div>
  </TextSurface>

  <script
    type="application/json"
    data-cart-catalogue
    is:inline
    set:html={catalogueJson}
  />

  <script
    is:inline
    define:vars={{
      locale,
      currency,
      shipping: SHIPPING,
      labels: {
        size: t(locale, "chooseSize"),
        quantity: t(locale, "cartQuantity"),
        remove: t(locale, "cartRemove"),
        onePiece: t(locale, "cartOnePiece"),
        free: t(locale, "cartShippingFree"),
        held: t(locale, "cartHeld"),
        sold: t(locale, "cartSold"),
        unavailable: t(locale, "cartUniqueOff"),
      },
    }}
  >
    (() => {
      const live = document.querySelector(".cart-live");
      const form = document.querySelector("[data-cart-form]");
      const list = document.querySelector("[data-cart-list]");
      const empty = document.querySelector("[data-cart-empty]");
      const totalOut = document.querySelector("[data-cart-total]");
      const source = document.querySelector("[data-cart-catalogue]");
      const paid = document.querySelector("[data-cart-paid]");
      const problem = document.querySelector("[data-cart-problem]");
      const payButton = document.querySelector("[data-pay-button]");
      const destField = document.querySelector("[data-destination-field]");
      const destination = document.querySelector("[data-destination]");
      const shipLine = document.querySelector("[data-shipping-line]");
      const shipOut = document.querySelector("[data-shipping-out]");
      const payLine = document.querySelector("[data-topay-line]");
      const payOut = document.querySelector("[data-topay-out]");
      if (!live || !form || !list || !empty || !source) return;

      const KEY = "ac-cart";
      const read = () => {
        try {
          const v = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) || "{}");
          return v && typeof v === "object" ? v : {};
        } catch {
          return {};
        }
      };
      const write = (v) => {
        try {
          localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(v));
        } catch {
          /* private mode: the page still works for this visit, it just forgets */
        }
        document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("ac-cart-changed"));
      };

      /*
        THE PAGE MUST NOT GO BLANK IF ANY OF THIS FAILS. The no-script sentence
        is hidden the moment scripts run, and the live block stays hidden until
        this script shows it — so one error would leave a heading over an empty
        page with no way on. Putting the sentence back is not a lie in that
        state: the cart really does not work, and where to go instead is the
        same answer either way.
      */
      const fail = (error) => {
        document.documentElement.classList.remove("js");
        live.hidden = true;
        console.error("[cart]", error);
      };

      let catalogue;
      try {
        catalogue = new Map(JSON.parse(source.textContent).map((i) => [i.slug, i]));
      } catch (error) {
        fail(error);
        return;
      }
      const fmt = new Intl.NumberFormat(locale === "it" ? "it-IT" : "en-GB", {
        style: "currency",
        currency,
        maximumFractionDigits: 0,
      });

      const el = (tag, className, text) => {
        const node = document.createElement(tag);
        if (className) node.className = className;
        if (text != null) node.textContent = text;
        return node;
      };

      /*
        THE LINES ARE BUILT AS NODES, NOT AS A STRING OF HTML. Every name here
        comes out of the dataset, which is the owner's to write; building the
        DOM means no piece can ever be named something that ends up parsed as
        markup, and there is no escaping rule for a later session to get wrong.

        THE CLASSES BELOW ONLY EVER EXIST INSIDE THIS SCRIPT, and they work
        because Tailwind scans this file as TEXT and finds them in here. It was
        checked in the compiled stylesheet rather than assumed. Two things
        follow, and they are the kind that fail silently: a class assembled from
        pieces (`"gap-u" + n`) would be found by nothing and would compile to
        nothing, and moving this script to its own .js file outside the content
        globs would take every rule here with it.
      */
      function line(slug, entry) {
        const item = catalogue.get(slug);
        if (!item) return null;

        const li = el("li", "cart-line");
        li.dataset.slug = slug;

        const left = el("div", "flex items-start gap-u3");
        if (item.image) {
          const img = document.createElement("img");
          img.src = item.image;
          img.alt = "";
          img.width = 60;
          img.height = 80;
          img.loading = "lazy";
          img.decoding = "async";
          img.style.objectFit = "cover";
          left.append(img);
        }

        const naming = el("div", "flex flex-col gap-u1");
        const link = el("a", "type-label text-label hairline-under self-start", item.name);
        link.href = item.href;
        naming.append(link, el("span", "type-mono text-mono", fmt.format(item.price)));
        if (item.max === 1) naming.append(el("span", "type-label text-label", labels.onePiece));
        /* Why this piece cannot be paid for, when it cannot. Written by `block()`. */
        const why = el("p", "type-body text-body max-w-prose");
        why.dataset.blocked = "";
        why.hidden = true;
        naming.append(why);
        left.append(naming);

        const controls = el("div", "flex flex-wrap items-end gap-u5");

        /* A piece he cuts in one size has nothing to choose (section 101). */
        const sizes = (item.sizes || []).filter((s) => s !== "ONE");
        if (sizes.length > 0) {
          const set = el("fieldset", "flex flex-col gap-u2");
          set.append(el("legend", "type-label text-label", labels.size));
          const row = el("div", "flex flex-wrap gap-u3");
          for (const size of sizes) {
            const label = el("label", "type-label text-label flex items-baseline gap-u1");
            const radio = document.createElement("input");
            radio.type = "radio";
            radio.name = `size:${slug}`;
            radio.value = size;
            radio.checked = entry.size === size;
            /*
              REQUIRED, AND NOTHING IS PICKED FOR THEM (section 130). The line
              used to fall back to the first size when nothing was stored, so a
              cart could carry XS for a piece whose size the buyer had never
              chosen. The browser now refuses to send until they answer, which
              is the same answer the piece page gives.
            */
            radio.required = true;
            label.append(radio, document.createTextNode(size));
            row.append(label);
          }
          set.append(row);
          controls.append(set);
        }

        const qtyField = el("div", "field");
        const qtyLabel = el("label", "type-label text-label", labels.quantity);
        qtyLabel.htmlFor = `qty-${slug}`;
        const qty = document.createElement("input");
        qty.id = `qty-${slug}`;
        qty.name = `qty:${slug}`;
        qty.type = "number";
        qty.inputMode = "numeric";
        qty.min = "1";
        /* A 1 of 1 stops at one, because one is all there is. */
        qty.max = String(item.max);
        qty.step = "1";
        qty.value = String(Math.min(Math.max(Number(entry.qty) || 1, 1), item.max));
        qtyField.append(qtyLabel, qty);
        controls.append(qtyField);

        const remove = el("button", "type-label text-label hairline-under", labels.remove);
        remove.type = "button";
        remove.dataset.removeSlug = slug;
        controls.append(remove);

        li.append(left, controls);
        return li;
      }

      function render() {
        const cart = read();
        list.replaceChildren();
        for (const [slug, entry] of Object.entries(cart)) {
          const node = line(slug, entry || {});
          if (node) list.append(node);
        }
        /*
          AND THEN WRITE BACK WHAT WAS BUILT, which is not a tidy-up: the lines
          above CLAMP. A stored quantity of 99 against a 1 of 1 renders as 1 and
          left storage saying 99, so the cart page showed one piece and the count
          in the chrome said 102 — measured, not imagined. A piece he has since
          sold or unpriced is dropped the same way and has to leave storage with
          it, or it comes back on the next page.

          What the reader sees is the truth here, so the truth is taken from the
          rendered lines rather than from what was stored.
        */
        sync(true);
        block();
        const has = list.children.length > 0;
        form.hidden = !has;
        empty.hidden = has;
        live.hidden = false;
      }

      /*
        WHAT THE READER TYPES IS WHAT IS KEPT. The controls in the list ARE the
        form fields, so there is nothing to copy across at submit time; this only
        mirrors them back into storage, so the count in the chrome agrees and the
        cart is still theirs on the next page.
      */
      function sync(correct) {
        const next = {};
        let total = 0;
        for (const li of list.querySelectorAll(".cart-line")) {
          const slug = li.dataset.slug;
          const item = catalogue.get(slug);
          if (!item) continue;
          const field = li.querySelector("input[type=number]");
          let qty = Math.floor(Number(field.value));
          if (!Number.isFinite(qty) || qty < 1) qty = 1;
          if (qty > item.max) qty = item.max;
          /*
            THE BOX IS CORRECTED WHEN THE READER LEAVES IT, NOT WHILE THEY TYPE.
            Clearing the box to type "12" empties it for one keystroke; writing
            "1" back into it at that instant fights the person using it. The
            total and the stored cart always use the clamped number, so nothing
            downstream ever sees the half-typed one.
          */
          if (correct && String(qty) !== field.value) field.value = String(qty);
          const size = li.querySelector("input[type=radio]:checked")?.value;
          next[slug] = size ? {qty, size} : {qty};
          total += qty * item.price;
        }
        write(next);
        if (totalOut) totalOut.textContent = fmt.format(total);
        goods = total;
        showShipping();
      }

      /*
        THE SHIPPING FIGURE, FROM THE SAME NUMBERS THE ENDPOINT USES.

        `shipping` here is src/lib/shipping.ts, handed to this script by the
        build and to /api/checkout through /order-catalogue.json. One table, two
        readers, so the figure shown on this page cannot differ from the figure
        Stripe is given — which is the only version of this feature worth
        having, because the difference between them would be money.

        It is DISPLAY ONLY. The endpoint recomputes all of it from the POST and
        would refuse a destination it does not ship to; nothing here is trusted
        by anything.
      */
      let goods = 0;
      function showShipping() {
        if (!destination || !shipLine || !payLine) return;
        const code = destination.value;
        const zone = shipping.europe.includes(code)
          ? "europe"
          : shipping.world.includes(code)
            ? "world"
            : null;
        /* No country, no honest number to print. */
        const known = Boolean(zone) && destField && !destField.hidden;
        shipLine.hidden = !known;
        payLine.hidden = !known;
        if (!known) return;
        const amount = goods >= shipping.freeFrom ? 0 : shipping.rates[zone];
        if (shipOut) shipOut.textContent = amount === 0 ? labels.free : fmt.format(amount);
        if (payOut) payOut.textContent = fmt.format(goods + amount);
      }
      if (destination) destination.addEventListener("change", showShipping);

      /*
        THE 1 OF 1 PIECES THE TILL WILL NOT TAKE, as /api/checkout reports them:
        sold, being paid for by somebody else this minute, or not lockable at
        all. Said beside the piece, and the card button goes while any of them
        is in the basket — pressing it would only come back refused.
      */
      let blocked = new Map();
      let cardOn = false;
      function block() {
        let any = false;
        for (const li of list.querySelectorAll(".cart-line")) {
          const why = blocked.get(li.dataset.slug);
          const note = li.querySelector("[data-blocked]");
          if (note) {
            note.hidden = !why;
            note.textContent = why ? labels[why] : "";
          }
          if (why) any = true;
        }
        if (payButton) payButton.hidden = !cardOn || any;
      }

      /*
        ON THE LIST, NOT ON THE FORM. The form also holds the destination, and
        listening there rewrote the stored cart on every change that does not
        belong to it. (It used to hold a name, an email and a note too; Stripe
        asks for those now.)
      */
      list.addEventListener("input", () => sync(false));
      list.addEventListener("change", () => sync(true));

      list.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
        const button = event.target.closest("[data-remove-slug]");
        if (!button) return;
        const cart = read();
        delete cart[button.dataset.removeSlug];
        write(cart);
        render();
      });

      /*
        THE SENDING STATE, the same enhancement every other form on this site
        has: the browser navigates on submit, and on a slow connection nothing
        appears to have happened, so a person presses again. The button is not
        disabled — a disabled control leaves the accessibility tree mid-action.
      */
      /*
        ENTER NEVER STARTS A PAYMENT. A form's implicit submission presses its
        first submit button when Enter is pressed in a field, and the only
        button here leaves for Stripe. Changing a quantity and pressing Enter
        is a person confirming a number, not choosing to pay, so it is caught
        here; the button, a click or a tap on it, is the one way to go.
      */
      form.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
        if (event.key !== "Enter") return;
        const target = event.target;
        if (target instanceof HTMLButtonElement || target instanceof HTMLTextAreaElement || target instanceof HTMLAnchorElement) return;
        event.preventDefault();
      });

      /*
        WHO IS ASKING, as far as the 1 of 1 lock is concerned. A random id kept
        in this browser, so that coming back from Stripe and pressing pay again
        takes back this browser's own hold instead of being told someone else
        is paying. Not a cookie and not sent anywhere but /api/checkout.
      */
      const holderField = form.querySelector("[data-holder]");
      if (holderField) {
        let id = "";
        try {
          id = localStorage.getItem("ac-holder") || "";
          if (!id) {
            id = crypto.randomUUID();
            localStorage.setItem("ac-holder", id);
          }
        } catch {
          /* private mode: an id for this page view, which still covers a double click */
          id = id || (crypto.randomUUID ? crypto.randomUUID() : "");
        }
        holderField.value = id;
      }

      form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
        /*
          THE BUTTON THAT WAS PRESSED, not the first one on the form. This read
          `querySelector("button[type=submit]")` while there was only one, and
          the moment a second arrived it would have put the sending state on the
          send button no matter which one the reader actually pressed.
        */
        const button = event.submitter || form.querySelector("button[type=submit]");
        if (!button) return;
        /* Once is enough: a second press while the first is on its way only races it. */
        if (form.dataset.busy) {
          event.preventDefault();
          return;
        }
        form.dataset.busy = "1";
        button.dataset.label = button.textContent;
        button.setAttribute("aria-busy", "true");
        button.textContent = button.dataset.sending;
      });

      /*
        BACK FROM STRIPE BY THE BACK BUTTON, the browser may hand back this very
        page from its cache, mid-submit: busy, and the button still saying it is
        sending. Put it back the way it was, or the buyer cannot press it again.
      */
      window.addEventListener("pageshow", (event) => {
        if (!event.persisted) return;
        delete form.dataset.busy;
        for (const b of form.querySelectorAll("button[aria-busy]")) {
          b.removeAttribute("aria-busy");
          if (b.dataset.label) b.textContent = b.dataset.label;
        }
      });

      const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);

      /*
        PAID, AND THEREFORE FINISHED. Stripe sends the buyer back here. Empty
        the basket, tell the chrome so its count follows, say thank you, and
        stop: there is no cart left on this page to render, no total to keep in
        step and no form to guard, so everything below would be machinery
        running over nothing.
      */
      if (params.has("paid") && paid) {
        try {
          localStorage.removeItem(KEY);
        } catch (error) {
          /* private mode: nothing was stored, so nothing needs removing */
        }
        document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("ac-cart-changed"));
        paid.hidden = false;
        form.hidden = true;
        empty.hidden = true;
        live.hidden = false;
        return;
      }

      if (params.has("problem") && problem) problem.hidden = false;

      /* Sent back by /api/checkout because a 1 of 1 in the basket was taken. */
      const refused = params.get("unavailable");
      if (refused && problem) {
        const why = ["held", "sold"].includes(params.get("why")) ? params.get("why") : "unavailable";
        const name = catalogue.get(refused)?.name;
        problem.textContent = name ? `${name}: ${labels[why]}` : labels[why];
        problem.hidden = false;
        blocked.set(refused, why);
      }

      /*
        IS THE CARD PATH ON? This page is static HTML written at build time and
        cannot see a Cloudflare environment variable; /api/checkout can, and
        answers {"enabled":true} only when both Stripe variables exist. So there
        is ONE switch — the project's environment — rather than a build flag and
        a runtime key free to disagree.

        EVERY FAILURE LEAVES THE BUTTON HIDDEN: a network error, a bad status, a
        body that is not what it should be. That is the direction this page
        already fails in everywhere else, and its reason holds here too — a
        control that cannot do its job is worse than no control, and a pay
        button that cannot take money is the worst of them.
      */
      fetch("/api/checkout", {headers: {Accept: "application/json"}})
        .then((res) => (res.ok ? res.json() : null))
        .then((info) => {
          if (!info || info.enabled !== true) return;
          cardOn = true;
          blocked = new Map();
          for (const slug of info.sold || []) blocked.set(slug, "sold");
          for (const slug of info.held || []) blocked.set(slug, "held");
          if (info.unique !== true) {
            for (const [slug, item] of catalogue) if (item.max === 1) blocked.set(slug, "unavailable");
          }
          block();
          if (destField) destField.hidden = false;
          showShipping();
        })
        .catch(() => {
          /* Dark, and that is the safe answer. */
        });

      try {
        render();
      } catch (error) {
        fail(error);
      }
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
